\begin_layout Section
Abstract
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Tennenbaum's theorem states that PA does not admit any nonstandard computable model.
 In 2022,
 Fedor Pakhomov proved that this theorem is fragile in regards to how PA is expressed,
 by constructing a theory that is definitionally equivalent to PA (roughly:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

it's PA but with a different choice of signature
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) for which there is a computable nonstandard model.
 I will introduce the audience to this result (and all necessary model-theoretic prerequisites) and,
 time allowing,
 present some original work improving Pakhomov's original construction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Review theory of Peano Arithmetic.
 As a fun exercise,
 ask the audience to explain it,
 to make the point that different people use different signatures.
 Maximal expected signature:
 
\begin_inset Formula $(0,1,S,+,\times,\leq)$
\end_inset

.
 Expect someone not to use 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
 Someone might want to add exponentiation?
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Axioms are things like:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall_{x}\forall_{y}(x+y=y+x)$
\end_inset

,
 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall_{x}\forall_{y}(x+Sy=S(x+y))$
\end_inset

,
 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall_{x}\forall_{y}(x\leq y\leftrightarrow\exists_{z}y=x+z)$
\end_inset

,
 and crucially,
 the axiom schema of induction:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(\varphi(0)\land\forall_{x}\varphi(x)\rightarrow\varphi(Sx)\right)\rightarrow\forall_{x}\varphi(x).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Anyway,
 make a point about how different people maybe write PA in a slightly different way.
 Hold that thought,
 it'll come up later.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
The naturals form a model of PA,
 but we can build models of PA that are 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 the natural numbers.
 Sketch:
 Consider PA plus a new constant symbol 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

,
 with axioms saying 
\begin_inset Formula $c\neq0$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $c\neq S0$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $c\neq SS0$
\end_inset

,
 etc.
 By compactness this set of axioms has a model.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 be its reduct to the signature of PA.
 Then this is a model of PA,
 so it acts like the natural numbers and satisfies induction,
 but also it has elements past all the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

true
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 natural numbers.
 Kind of wild.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
There's a cool book by Kaye called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Models of Peano Arithmetic
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 that studies these nonstandard models.
 There's an impressive amount you can say about them.
 But one thing that it seems you 
\emph on
cannot
\emph default
 do,
 is you cannot show your friend one of these models very explicitly,
 because they're pretty complicated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Theorem:
 (Tennenbaum) If 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 is a nonstandard model of PA,
 neither 
\begin_inset Formula $+^{M}$
\end_inset

 nor 
\begin_inset Formula $\times^{M}$
\end_inset

 are computable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Put a different way:
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $M=\{a_{0},a_{1},\dots\}$
\end_inset

 is a countable nonstandard model of PA,
 then the function 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{add}\colon\N^{2}\to\N$
\end_inset

 given by 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}+a_{j}=a_{\mathrm{add}(i,j)}$
\end_inset

 is not computable,
 no matter how the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 have been put in bijection with the naturals,
 and likewise for multiplication.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Proof strategy:
 Encode a completion of the universal partial computable function into a nonstandard element,
 as a tuple.
 It takes some work,
 but is possible to show,
 that you can do this in such a way that you only need to know addition (or multiplication) to decode this completion,
 thereby allowing you to compute such a completion,
 which by a diagonalization argument cannot be computable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
You need very little induction to make this work,
 as it turns out.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{1}$
\end_inset

 induction suffices for this argument for sure,
 and you can actually get it to work with bounded induction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Okay,
 so this is a background theorem and much ink has been spilled about it.
 People have built a zillion variants of this theorem for weaker fragments of PA,
 as well as for other related theories,
 like 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

finite set theory
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Details on 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

finite set theory
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

:
 ZF,
 minus infinity,
 plus 
\begin_inset Formula $\in$
\end_inset

-induction.
 This is in a paper by Mancini and Zambella.
 They also show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\in$
\end_inset

-induction is necessary.
