\begin_layout Section
Definitional Equivalence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Let's talk a bit more about this finite set theory business:
 It makes sense that Tennenbaum would work there,
 because FST is,
 in some sense,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

PA in disguise
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 People in the audience might know about 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Ackermann's membership predicate
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{\in}$
\end_inset

,
 which imbues 
\begin_inset Formula $\N$
\end_inset

 with the structure of a model of finite set theory,
 and if you have a model of FST you can also define ways to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

add and multiply
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 sets that gives you a model of PA.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
This is an example of a notion called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

definitional equivalence
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Definition:
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is a theory over the signature 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lang$
\end_inset

,
 a 
\emph on
definitional extension of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset


\emph default
 is a theory 
\begin_inset Formula $T'\supseteq T$
\end_inset

 over the signature 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lang'\supseteq\Lang$
\end_inset

 that satisfies the two conditions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Every new symbol can be defined in terms of the previous.
 This means:
 For every predicate symbol 
\begin_inset Formula $P(\vec{x})\in\Lang'$
\end_inset

,
 there is a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lang$
\end_inset

-formula 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi_{P}(\vec{x})$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
T'\vdash\forall_{\vec{x}}\left(P(\vec{x})\leftrightarrow\varphi_{P}(\vec{x})\right),
\]

\end_inset

and something similar for function symbols.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $T'$
\end_inset

 is a conservative extension of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

:
 For every 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lang$
\end_inset

-sentence 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi$
\end_inset

,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $T'\vdash\varphi$
\end_inset

 then it must also be that 
\begin_inset Formula $T\vdash\varphi$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Definition:
 Two theories 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 over the (wlog disjoint) signatures 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lang_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lang_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 are said to be 
\emph on
definitionally equivalent
\emph default
 if they admit a common definitional extension.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
This is also sometimes called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

synonymy
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 as in:
 The two theories are said to be synonymous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Remark:
 This is an equivalence relation on theories.
 It is related to,
 and strictly stronger than,
 bi-interpretability,
 though the two notions often coincide.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
For those who know bi-interpretability:
 There,
 you interpret one theory inside the other,
 and require that when you compose the two you get a definable subset of the original.
 For definitional equivalence,
 you add the requirement that you get 
\emph on
all
\emph default
 of the original.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Example:
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{0}$
\end_inset

 be an axiomatization of PA using 
\begin_inset Formula $(0,1,+,\times,\leq)$
\end_inset

,
 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{1}$
\end_inset

 be an axiomatization of PA using 
\begin_inset Formula $(0,S,+,\times)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Less trivial example:
 (Kaye–Wong,
 2007) Let 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{0}$
\end_inset

 be your favorite axiomatization of PA,
 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{1}$
\end_inset

 be:
 ZF,
 with infinity replaced by its negation,
 plus the axiom of transitive closure:
 Every set is contained in a transitive set.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Equivalently to TC:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\in$
\end_inset

-induction,
 or 
\begin_inset Formula $V=\cup_{\alpha\in\mathrm{Ord}}V_{\alpha}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Without the axiom of TC,
 we get bi-interpretability but not definitional equivalence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Question (Pakhomov,
 November 2021):
 Does Tennenbaum's theorem hold for every theory that is definitionally equivalent to PA?
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Works for PA,
 works for FST,
 doesn't work for FST without TC,
 so it makes sense.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Important remark:
 We will be working for FST instead of PA.
 This is not an essential difference b/c these are def.
 eqv.
 which is a transitive relation,
 but the fact that FST has a single relational predicate makes things technically easier.
