\begin_layout Section
Escaping Tennenbaum's Theorem:
 The Goal
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Theorem (Pakhomov,
 September 2022):
 There is a theory which is definitionally equivalent to PA/FST that admits computable nonstandard models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
I will sketch Pakhomov's construction now,
 but first,
 some background in computable structure theory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
One easy fact about nonstandard models of PA is the following:
 Even though none of them is computable,
 there are many which are 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable.
 (Even low,
 but we won't be using that here.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Moreover,
 there is a class of theorems in CST,
 called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

strong jump inversion theorems
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 of the following form:
 If [structure] has a 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable copy,
 then [reduct] has a computable copy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Some examples:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(L,\leq,S)$
\end_inset

 be a linear order,
 whose every element admits an immediate successor and predecessor,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $S(x,y)$
\end_inset

 being the successor predicate.
 Then,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $(L,\leq,S)$
\end_inset

 admits a 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable copy,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $(L,\leq)$
\end_inset

 admits a computable copy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(E,\simeq,P_{1},P_{2},\cdots)$
\end_inset

 be an equivalence relation with infinitely many infinite equivalence classes,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{n}(x)$
\end_inset

 is the predicate 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

equivalence class of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 has size 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 Then,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $(E,\simeq,P_{1},P_{2},\dots)$
\end_inset

 admits a 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable copy,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $(E,\simeq)$
\end_inset

 admits a computable copy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Now,
 Pakhomov didn't phrase it this way,
 but if you read his paper with an eye for it,
 you'll be able to interpret his work roughly as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Theorem:
 It is possible to define in FST a ternary predicate 
\begin_inset Formula $S(x,y,z)$
\end_inset

,
 such that the 
\begin_inset Formula $\in$
\end_inset

 relation is definable in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 and,
 for any 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable model 
\begin_inset Formula $(M,\in,S)$
\end_inset

 of FST,
 the reduct 
\begin_inset Formula $(M,S)$
\end_inset

 admits a computable copy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Corollary:
 The theory 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{S}$
\end_inset

 of the predicate 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 is definitionally equivalent to FST and hence PA.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Corollary:
 There is a computable nonstandard model of 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{S}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Proof:
 Use the computable completeness theorem to obtain a 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable model of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{FST}+S$
\end_inset

,
 and use the jump inversion theorem to obtain a computable model of 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{S}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Maybe this is a good place to pause.
 This is the entire point of the talk,
 so make sure everyone is on the same page about what's happening.
