\begin_layout Point
In a similar fashion,
 we can build a picture for what 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable models look like.
 Of course,
 we can just relativize the previous definition to say that it's a machine,
 with access to an oracle for 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

,
 that outputs elements and their relations,
 but for our purposes it's useful to do something a la Shoenfield's limit lemma:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Concept:
 A 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable model 
\begin_inset Formula $(M,\{R_{0},R_{1},\ldots\})$
\end_inset

 is a Turing machine that will sometimes output 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

here is a new element and its relations to the previous ones
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 and will sometimes output 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

oops never mind
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
A little more precisely:
 Sometimes will output 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

forget about the last 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 elements I added
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
This vision doesn't quite work,
 as stated,
 for infinite signatures.
 In my paper I introduce something called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable c.e.-typed structures
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to get past this difficulty.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
With that in mind,
 a strong jump inversion theorem can be seen as a way to recover from mistakes.
 For example,
 the jump inversion theorem for equivalence relations works roughly like the following:
 If you have a 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable copy of your equivalence relation,
 you simply copy that,
 but if you find that an element you've added along the way is actually FAKE NEWS,
 you put it in the punishment corner with all the other misbehaving elements,
 and turn it into an infinite equivalence class.
 Since there are already infinitely many of those,
 this doesn't change the isomorphism class.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
The main obstacle in jump inversion constructions of this type is the following:
 How do you recover from adding an element that should never have been there?
