\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Pakhomov's idea was to build a predicate in FST that has enough flexibility to recover from any reasonable mistake that you make,
 whilst having enough structure to encode the membership relation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Goal:
 Build a ternary predicate 
\begin_inset Formula $S(x,y,z)$
\end_inset

 that satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in y\leftrightarrow\forall_{z}S(x,y,z)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
We do this by iterating in the von
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

Neumann hierarchy.
 Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 has been defined up to 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{\alpha}$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
 imagine all possible ways that a new element 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 could 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

-relate to the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{\alpha}$
\end_inset

,
 and for each such hypothetical way,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

schedule
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 an element to fit that bill.
 This element will not be 
\begin_inset Formula $\in$
\end_inset

-related to any element of 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{\alpha}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Pakhomov's original construction actually adds this element to 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{\alpha+6}$
\end_inset

,
 but in my paper I used a more dynamic scheduling instead.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Note that,
 when we say 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

all possible ways that 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 could 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

-relate to the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{\alpha}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 this is subject to the restriction 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in y\rightarrow\forall_{z}S(x,y,z)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Example:
 Consider 
