\begin_layout Section
Original Work
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
In his original paper,
 Pakhomov left a couple of questions unanswered.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
One of these questions was answered by Patrick Lutz and James Walsh in 2023:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Question (Pakhomov):
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 be a c.e.
\begin_inset space \space{}
\end_inset

theory with no computable models.
 Must there necessarily be a definitionally equivalent theory 
\begin_inset Formula $T'$
\end_inset

 admitting computable models?
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
You should think of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 as:
 PA + there is a nonstandard element.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Answer (Lutz–Walsh):
 No.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
The other question takes a little more effort to motivate.
 Besides showing that PA is able to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

escape Tennenbaum
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 via definitional equivalence,
 Pakhomov was able to show that True Arithmetic is now:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Theorem (Pakhomov):
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is definitionally equivalent to True Arithmetic,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 admits no computable (or even arithmetical) nonstandard models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Sketch:
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is definitionally equivalent to TA,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $+$
\end_inset

 is definable in 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 by a first-order formula.
 This formula will have some finite number of quantifiers,
 say 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

.
 Now,
 the same strategy used in the proof of Tennenbaum's theorem can be used to show that a nonstandard model of TA admits a nonstandard element 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 that encodes,
 say,
 
\begin_inset Formula $0^{(n+1)}$
\end_inset

,
 and thus we can decode 
\begin_inset Formula $0^{(n+1)}$
\end_inset

 using a formula with 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 quantifiers,
 which only requires 
\begin_inset Formula $d^{(n)}$
\end_inset

 computational power where 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

 is the degree of the model.
 Thus,
 if there were a computable nonstandard model,
 
\begin_inset Formula $0^{(n)}$
\end_inset

 would be able to compute 
\begin_inset Formula $0^{(n+1)}$
\end_inset

,
 a contradiction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Question (Pakhomov):
 We know that PA is capable of escaping Tennenbaum,
 but True Arithmetic is not.
 What about PA + 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{n}$
\end_inset

 truths,
 for varying values of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Theorem (M.):
 For every 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

,
 there is a theory that is definitionally equivalent to PA + 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{n}$
\end_inset

 truths,
 that admits computable nonstandard models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
The proof consisted of stacking Pakhomov's idea on top of itself,
 building recursively more complex predicates 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{2}(x,y,z,w)$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{3}(x,y,z,w,k)$
\end_inset

,
 etc.
\begin_inset space \space{}
\end_inset

each of which satisfies this 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

it's the previous one with witnesses
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 condition,
 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

generic trash in the last component
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 condition.
 Then,
 I proved a jump inversion type theorem that let you 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

lose one of these predicates and gain a jump of computational power
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 and finally one uses the fact that PA + 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{n}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $0^{(n)}$
\end_inset

-c.e.
\begin_inset space \space{}
\end_inset

 to obtain a 
\begin_inset Formula $0^{(n+1)}$
\end_inset

-computable nonstandard model with 
\begin_inset Formula $n+2$
\end_inset

 of these predicates,
 and then jump invert 
\begin_inset Formula $n+1$
\end_inset

 times to get a computable model implementing 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{n+1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
In the process,
 Patrick Lutz looked at the proof and suggested that it could be generalized.
 That turned out to be very fruitful,
 and so in the final paper I end up proving a very general Strong Jump Inversion type theorem,
 from which many existing results can be obtained as corollaries.
 That could be a talk of its own,
 but it's very technical so it would probably not be a very good talk.
 But it's something unexpected that came out of this project.
\end_layout

